WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.802
Welcome to Lab 3.

2
00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.805
We're now ready to build an intelligent document analysis agent

3
00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:09.909
that combines OCR, layout detection, and VLM tools.

4
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.078
You'll follow an agentic approach,

5
00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:15.582
which allows you to recognize different content types automatically,

6
00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:17.884
apply the right tools for each content type,

7
00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:20.887
and combine insights into coherent answers.

8
00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.590
This is similar to how a human analyst

9
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.492
would approach a complex report,

10
00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:27.594
scanning the structure first,

11
00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:30.797
then diving deep into specific sections as needed.

12
00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.366
The overall pipeline will have three components.

13
00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:35.268
First, text extraction.

14
00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:37.971
We'll use PaddleOCR for text extraction

15
00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:40.273
and LayoutLM for reading order.

16
00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:42.275
Second, layout detection.

17
00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:46.279
PaddleOCR layout detection to detect tables, charts, and regions.

18
00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:48.481
Third, agentic processing.

19
00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:51.985
We'll use a LangChain agent with two specialized tools.

20
00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:56.189
AnalyzeChart, a VLM-based chart figure analysis.

21
00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:59.192
AnalyzeTable, a VLM table extraction.

22
00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:02.495
Let's begin by loading the environment variables

23
00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:04.497
from a .env file.

24
00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:08.201
This file contains the API key for OpenAI,

25
00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:11.204
which we will need later when we define the agent.

26
00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:15.108
Next, we import the essential libraries for our pipeline.

27
00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.511
PIL for loading and manipulating images,

28
00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:21.781
cv2 from OpenCV for image processing

29
00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:23.483
and drawing bounding boxes,

30
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.386
matplotlib for visualizing the result.

31
00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:30.690
numpy for numerical operations on image arrays,

32
00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:34.094
dataclass, Python's built-in decorator

33
00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:36.696
for creating clean data structures.

34
00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:41.000
typing for type hints that make our code more readable.

35
00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.937
With our environment set up, let's dive

36
00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:46.005
into the first stage of our pipeline:

37
00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.208
extracting text from the document

38
00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:51.711
and determining the correct reading order using LayoutLM.

39
00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:56.382
First, we create a PaddleOCR instance configured for English text.

40
00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.185
Then we load our sample document,

41
00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:03.656
which is the same economics report you've seen in the previous lab.

42
00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:06.960
Now, let's run the OCR engine on our document.

43
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.662
As you've already seen in the previous lab,

44
00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:12.866
the OCR engine returns three key pieces of information

45
00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:16.870
for each detected region: the recognized text strings,

46
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:20.273
the confidence score, and bounding box coordinates.

47
00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.575
Visual verification is important.

48
00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:27.280
Here, we draw green bounding boxes around each detected text region.

49
00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.316
This helps us confirm that OCR is

50
00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:32.986
correctly identifying all text areas in the document.

51
00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.288
Notice how each line of text,

52
00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:38.658
table cell, and label gets its own bounding box.

53
00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:42.795
Raw OCR output is just list of values.

54
00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:45.698
To make our code cleaner and more maintainable,

55
00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:48.601
we create an OCR region data class.

56
00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:52.305
This gives us a clean type structure for each text region

57
00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:55.909
and a convenient bounding box XY XY property

58
00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:59.012
that converts the four corner polygon to a simple

59
00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:02.115
X1 Y1 X2 Y2 format.

60
00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:06.653
This structure format will be used throughout the rest of our pipeline.

61
00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.656
Now that we have the raw OCR output,

62
00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:12.358
we need to determine the correct reading order.

63
00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:16.863
Let's use LayoutReader based on LayoutLMv3 for this task.

64
00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:20.667
Let's load the pre-trained LayoutReader model from Hugging Face.

65
00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.671
This model takes bounding box coordinates as input

66
00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:27.640
and predicts the reading order position for each box.

67
00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:31.644
The model has been trained to understand common document layouts

68
00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:36.649
including single column, multi-column, tables, and mixed layouts.

69
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.219
With the model loaded, let's define

70
00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:41.754
the core function for reading order detection.

71
00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:44.524
Here is what it does step by step.

72
00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:47.126
First, we calculate the image dimensions.

73
00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:52.232
We estimate the image size from the bounding boxes with 10% padding.

74
00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.834
Second, we normalize the coordinates.

75
00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:59.072
LayoutLM expects coordinates in a 0 to 1000 range.

76
00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:01.274
So we scale our bounding boxes.

77
00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:03.776
Third, we prepare the inputs.

78
00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:07.080
We convert to the format that the transformer expects.

79
00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.284
Fourth, we run inference and get the model's prediction.

80
00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.287
Last but not least, we parse the results.

81
00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:17.890
We extract the reading order from the model's output logits.

82
00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:20.827
The result is a list that tells us

83
00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:23.196
the reading order for each OCR text region.

84
00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:25.565
Let's see the reading order in action.

85
00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:29.669
This visualization overlays numbers on each text region,

86
00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.672
showing the sequence in which they should be read.

87
00:04:33.273 --> 00:04:35.975
The numbers in red indicate the reading position.

88
00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:39.946
Notice the logical flow. Title first, then the content.

89
00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.283
following the natural way a human would read the document.

90
00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:46.886
Of course, it's not perfect with some jumping around,

91
00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:49.088
and this shows some of the

92
00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:51.190
limitations you may face with this approach.

93
00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:53.660
And depending on the complexity, you may

94
00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.695
have to fine-tune your own layout model

95
00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.497
for chunking and reading order.

96
00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.733
which can be hard to develop, scale, and maintain.

97
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.337
Finally, let's combine our OCR text with the reading order

98
00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:06.739
to produce properly sequenced text.

99
00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:09.709
This function pairs each OCR region

100
00:05:09.709 --> 00:05:11.577
with the corresponding reading position.

101
00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:14.814
Then, it sorts all regions by their position.

102
00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:18.651
and returns a list of dictionaries with position, text,

103
00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:21.054
confidence, and bounding box info.

104
00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:24.957
This ordered text will become part of our agent's context,

105
00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:27.393
allowing it to understand the document

106
00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.662
content without needing to call a VLM

107
00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.964
for basic text questions.

108
00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:34.367
Now that we can extract and order text,

109
00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:38.237
we need to understand what types of content exist in the document.

110
00:05:38.738 --> 00:05:40.840
This is where layout detection comes in.

111
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.642
As you've seen in the previous lab,

112
00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:46.245
PaddleOCR provides a separate LayoutDetection class

113
00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:49.248
specifically for identifying document structure.

114
00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:51.751
Let's initialize it now.

115
00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:54.153
Next, we define the process_document function

116
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:58.524
to run layout detection and return a list of detected regions.

117
00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:00.827
Each region includes the label,

118
00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:05.832
the type of content, like text, table, chart, figure, etc.

119
00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:09.135
The score, confidence score for the detection.

120
00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:13.573
bbox, bounding box coordinates in XYXY format.

121
00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:16.976
Let's take a closer look at the top five detected regions.

122
00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:19.479
Notice the different content types.

123
00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:22.782
You have text blocks, chart, and paragraph title.

124
00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:25.385
Similar to our OCR results,

125
00:06:25.485 --> 00:06:27.787
let's create a LayoutRegion dataclass

126
00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:29.889
for clean data handling.

127
00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:32.792
Each region will get a unique ID

128
00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:35.762
that our tools need to reference specific regions.

129
00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:38.498
Then we'll loop through each result

130
00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:40.400
and store it in a structured format.

131
00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.303
Now let's visualize all the layout regions with color-coded boxes.

132
00:06:44.804 --> 00:06:46.606
Each region type gets a unique color.

133
00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:51.177
and we display the region ID, type, and confidence score.

134
00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:54.881
As you can see, we identified titles, tables,

135
00:06:54.981 --> 00:06:56.983
charts, and bodies of text.

136
00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:59.085
With our layout regions identified, we

137
00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:01.187
need to prepare them for the agent.

138
00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.990
For our agent to analyze a chart or table,

139
00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.993
we need to send a cropped region to the VLM.

140
00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:11.364
This approach has several benefits. First is focused analysis.

141
00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:14.834
The VLM only sees the relevant content.

142
00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:17.036
Second is reduced noise.

143
00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:19.238
Surrounding text does not interfere.

144
00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.441
Third is lower costs.

145
00:07:21.541 --> 00:07:23.943
Smaller images mean lower API cost.

146
00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:27.814
Now let's crop each region using the crop_region function.

147
00:07:28.214 --> 00:07:31.517
We also convert images to base64 encoding,

148
00:07:31.717 --> 00:07:34.220
which is the format vision APIs expect.

149
00:07:34.821 --> 00:07:37.824
Then let's load the image and use these two functions

150
00:07:38.024 --> 00:07:42.528
to create a dictionary containing information for each cropped region.

151
00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:45.431
Keep in mind that even though VLMs are powerful,

152
00:07:46.132 --> 00:07:50.136
they're not great at localization. This approach may help improve accuracy,

153
00:07:50.436 --> 00:07:53.840
but you'll need to fine-tune your prompts for various edge cases.

154
00:07:54.173 --> 00:07:56.742
to deal with greater complexities and variants.

155
00:07:57.243 --> 00:07:59.745
Let's display all the crop regions to see

156
00:08:00.046 --> 00:08:02.648
what content the agent will have access to.

157
00:08:03.049 --> 00:08:06.352
Each region is labeled with its ID and type.

158
00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:09.021
Now that we have the text

159
00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:11.457
extracted and ordered, and the layout detected,

160
00:08:11.457 --> 00:08:14.260
We can start building our agent, starting with the tools.

161
00:08:14.861 --> 00:08:17.430
We'll create two specialized tools, each designed

162
00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.966
for a specific type of content analysis.

163
00:08:20.466 --> 00:08:25.466
AnalyzeChart uses a vision language model to interpret charts and figures.

164
00:08:25.972 --> 00:08:27.974
AnalyzeTable, also uses

165
00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:31.410
the VLM to extract structured data from tables.

166
00:08:31.711 --> 00:08:35.014
By creating specialized tools, we can use optimized prompts

167
00:08:35.014 --> 00:08:37.049
for each content type and return

168
00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.418
structured JSON that is easy to process.

169
00:08:39.819 --> 00:08:44.323
For VLM, we will use gpt-4o-mini from OpenAI.

170
00:08:44.557 --> 00:08:47.159
Next, let's define the prompts for each tool.

171
00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.363
The prompts we use are critical for getting useful,

172
00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:52.331
structured output from the VLM.

173
00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:54.734
Each prompt will have three components.

174
00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:57.169
First, we'll define the role.

175
00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.172
In this case, you are a chart analysis specialist.

176
00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:02.675
Second, we'll define what to extract.

177
00:09:02.775 --> 00:09:06.178
specific fields like chart type, axes, and data points.

178
00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:10.983
Then we'll provide a JSON template to ensure consistent output format.

179
00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:14.020
Well-defined prompts will help ensure the VLM

180
00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.656
returns data our agent can reliably use.

181
00:09:16.956 --> 00:09:19.659
We'll use the same logic for the second prompt.

182
00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:22.528
Before creating our tools, let's define a utility function

183
00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:26.933
that handles the mechanics of calling the VLM with an image.

184
00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:31.537
It creates a multimodal message containing both the text prompt

185
00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:33.940
and the base64 encoded image.

186
00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:36.242
Then returns the model's response.

187
00:09:36.742 --> 00:09:38.945
The @tool decorator from LangChain

188
00:09:38.945 --> 00:09:42.214
transforms our function into an agent usable tool.

189
00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:44.517
It takes in the region_id,

190
00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:48.521
checks if the region exists using the dictionary of regions.

191
00:09:48.521 --> 00:09:50.723
we just created, get the region data,

192
00:09:50.723 --> 00:09:54.327
and then passes the cropped image in base64

193
00:09:54.327 --> 00:09:56.829
and the corresponding prompt to the VLM.

194
00:09:57.129 --> 00:10:01.133
Following the same pattern, let's create our second tool for table extraction.

195
00:10:01.634 --> 00:10:04.537
It uses the table-specific prompt to guide the VLM

196
00:10:04.537 --> 00:10:06.739
in extracting structured data

197
00:10:06.739 --> 00:10:09.141
with proper headers and row organization.

198
00:10:09.542 --> 00:10:12.144
Before integrating our tools into the agent,

199
00:10:12.144 --> 00:10:14.046
let's test them individually.

200
00:10:14.046 --> 00:10:17.350
This verifies that the VLM connection works and our prompts

201
00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:21.387
produce useful output. We'll find the first chart region and analyze it.

202
00:10:21.787 --> 00:10:25.291
Notice that the data points are close but not 100% accurate.

203
00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:27.927
This is due to a lack of

204
00:10:27.927 --> 00:10:30.496
visual reasoning and localization capabilities I mentioned earlier.

205
00:10:30.997 --> 00:10:32.898
Similarly, let's test the table tool.

206
00:10:33.199 --> 00:10:35.401
This tool does a decent job of

207
00:10:35.401 --> 00:10:37.603
extracting the information from this simple table.

208
00:10:38.104 --> 00:10:42.475
However, as complexity grows, the agent may have a harder time localizing,

209
00:10:42.975 --> 00:10:45.778
extracting, and may be prone to hallucination.

210
00:10:46.145 --> 00:10:48.247
With our tools tested and working,

211
00:10:48.247 --> 00:10:51.751
we're ready to build the agent that will orchestrate everything.

212
00:10:52.051 --> 00:10:53.653
So how does the agent work?

213
00:10:54.053 --> 00:10:57.156
First, the agent will receive a question about the document.

214
00:10:57.556 --> 00:10:59.659
Second, it reads the system prompt

215
00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:02.928
containing all OCR text and the layout info.

216
00:11:03.329 --> 00:11:06.132
Third, it will decide whether or not

217
00:11:06.132 --> 00:11:09.101
it can answer from text alone or need to use a tool.

218
00:11:09.301 --> 00:11:12.805
Fourth, for visual content, like charts and tables,

219
00:11:13.105 --> 00:11:15.107
it will call the appropriate tool.

220
00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:18.310
And last but not least, it will combine all the information

221
00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:20.513
into a coherent response.

222
00:11:21.013 --> 00:11:25.017
Before building the agent, let's verify that our data structures are ready.

223
00:11:25.117 --> 00:11:27.920
We need both the ordered text from the OCR

224
00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.522
plus the LayoutLM.

225
00:11:29.789 --> 00:11:32.591
and the layout regions from layout detection.

226
00:11:32.992 --> 00:11:35.594
Now, let's prepare the context for our agent.

227
00:11:35.995 --> 00:11:38.597
The agent needs a well-formatted system prompt.

228
00:11:38.798 --> 00:11:43.002
So these helper functions convert our data structures into readable text

229
00:11:43.002 --> 00:11:44.603
as strings.

230
00:11:44.804 --> 00:11:49.175
The formatted context serves as the agent's memory of the document.

231
00:11:49.675 --> 00:11:51.777
Now we construct the system prompt,

232
00:11:51.977 --> 00:11:54.580
which is the foundation of our agent's behavior.

233
00:11:54.847 --> 00:11:56.849
It contains the role definition,

234
00:11:56.949 --> 00:12:00.352
in this case you are a document intelligence agent,

235
00:12:00.653 --> 00:12:04.123
the document context, all OCR text in reading order,

236
00:12:04.523 --> 00:12:07.626
the layout information, region types and IDs,

237
00:12:07.927 --> 00:12:10.329
tool descriptions, when to use each tool.

238
00:12:10.596 --> 00:12:14.400
and the instructions, how to handle different content types.

239
00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:16.802
Finally, let's put it all together.

240
00:12:17.103 --> 00:12:20.806
We'll use LangChain's create_tool_calling_agent to build our agent.

241
00:12:21.407 --> 00:12:23.109
This involves tools list,

242
00:12:23.509 --> 00:12:26.412
our AnalyzeChart and AnalyzeTable functions.

243
00:12:26.612 --> 00:12:30.082
The LLM, gpt-4o-mini for cost efficiency.

244
00:12:30.382 --> 00:12:33.385
The prompt template, which combines system prompt,

245
00:12:33.786 --> 00:12:36.088
user input, and scratchpad.

246
00:12:36.522 --> 00:12:39.125
Then, we'll create an agent that can call tools.

247
00:12:39.825 --> 00:12:42.828
Last but not least, we'll set up the AgentExecutor

248
00:12:42.828 --> 00:12:46.298
to run the tool-enabled loop. We'll set verbose=True,

249
00:12:46.632 --> 00:12:49.902
which is a setting that lets us see the agent's reasoning process.

250
00:12:50.503 --> 00:12:53.105
With our agent assembled, let's put it to the test.

251
00:12:53.539 --> 00:12:56.342
First, we'll ask a general question about the document.

252
00:12:56.542 --> 00:12:59.545
This should be answerable from the OCR text alone,

253
00:12:59.612 --> 00:13:01.347
without calling any tools.

254
00:13:01.747 --> 00:13:04.750
Watch how the agent uses its context to respond.

255
00:13:05.484 --> 00:13:08.988
Now let's ask the agent to extract table data.

256
00:13:09.388 --> 00:13:11.690
Watch how it identifies the table region

